Abstract
Due to their attractive optoelectronic properties, metal halide APbI3 perovskites employing formamidinum $(\mathrm{FA^{+}})$ as A cation are presently the focus of intense research, The superior chemical and thermal stability of $\mathrm{FA}^{+}$ cations renders $a{\mathrm{-FAPbI}}_{3}$ more suitable for solar cell applications than methylammonium lead iodide (MAPbI3). However, its spontaneous conversion to the yellow nonperovskite phase $\mathit{\Delta}_{\leftmoon}\delta$ -FAPbI3) under ambient conditions poses a serious challenge for practical applications. Here, we report on the stabilization of the desired $a{\mathrm{-FAPbI}}_{3}$ perovskite phase by protecting it with a two-dimensional (2D) $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ $\mathbf{\vec{\tau}}_{\mathrm{IBA}}=i s o$ -butylammonium overlayer, formed via stepwise annealing. Remarkably, the $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI_{3}/I B A_{2}F A P b_{2}I_{7}}}$ based perovskite solar cell (PSC) reached a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of close to $23\%$ . In addition, it showed excellent operational stability, retaining ${\sim}85\%$ of its initial efficiency under severe combined heat and light stress, i.e. simultaneous exposure with maximum power tracking to full simulated sunlight at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ over a period of $500\mathrm{h}$ .
## Introduction
Due to their high power conversion efficiency (PCE) exceeding already $25\%$ and ease of production, hybrid organic metal halide perovskite solar cells (PSCs) are presently attracting wide attention both from the scientific community and photovoltaic industry (1-8). Most PSCs employ formulations containing methylammonium $(\mathrm{MA^{+}})$ cations which however are unstable decomposing to methylamine upon exposure to heat, moisture, and light (9-14). Therefore, for the large-scale deployment of highly-efficient PSCs, the intrinsic instability issues associated with the loss of $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ cations need to be mitigated (7, 15-21). In this regard, formamidinium $(\mathrm{FA^{+}})$ cation offers an attractive alternative to $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ as pristine FAPbI3 features lower volatility and close to optimal
Goldschmidt tolerance factor (22). as well as an absorption edge extending into the near IR to ${\sim}840$ nm. rendering pristine FAPbI3 a more efficient solar light than MAPbI3 (4, 23-25). However, the spontaneous formation of the photovoltaically inactive, non-perovskite yellow phase $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{S}}$ -FAPbI3) below the $\ensuremath{\alpha}$ to $\delta$ phase transition at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\left(I7,26\right)$ causes a serious predicament. introducing ${\mathrm{Cs}}^{+}$ , or $\mathrm{MA}^{+}$ into the $\mathrm{FAPb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ lattice prevents the formation of its delta phase, However, the incorporation of these ions affects the spectral response and operational stability of the resulting PSCs under heat stress and in humid conditions (4, 27-29).
Here we achieve stabilization of the $a\mathrm{-FAPbI}_{3}$ phase by introducing a stepwise annealing process and covering it with a novel two-dimensional $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}\left(\mathrm{n}=2\right)$ perovskite layer containing isobutylammonium (IBA) as a spacer layer. The confluence of stepwise annealing and $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ lamination allowed the formation of pristine $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI}}_{3}$ films containing long-lasting charge-carriers with lifetime exceeding $1.5~{\upmu\mathrm{s}}$ . These desired structural and spectral features translated into high photovoltage of $1113\mathrm{mV}$ as compared to $1053\mathrm{mV}$ for reference, leading to the realization of a PCE close to $23\%$ , one of a record for FAPbI3 –based PSCs. Remarkably we achieved excellent operational stability, the PSCs retaining ${\sim}95\%$ of their initial efficiency at the maximum power point (MPP) under full-sun illumination over $700\mathrm{{h}}$ . The superior robustness of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ protected by the 2D overlayer was further confirmed by long term testing under harsh simultaneous heat and light stress. These devices showed only small degradation under full-sun illumination for 500 hours at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , while unprotected $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ films lost rapidly over $60\%$ of their initial performance.
