X-ray reflectivity (XRR) data of pristine and IBAI treated FAPbI3 films are shown in Figure 2c. Each film exhibits structural features corresponding to the $\boldsymbol{a}$ -FAPbI3 perovskite phase with a lattice spacing of $6.36\mathrm{\AA}$ (32), the hexagonal $\mathrm{FAPb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ (non-perovskite phase) (33) and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ . The IBAI treated FAPbI3 films revealed the formation of $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ overlayer exhibiting reflections of the 2D-structure with a lattice spacing of $38.56\mathrm{\AA}$ (Table 1). The lattice spacing is slightly smaller than the lattice spacing of the ${\mathrm{BA}}_{2}{\mathrm{FAPb}}_{2}{\mathrm{I}}_{7}$ $(\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}=2)$ ) structure of 39.35 Å (34), which is consistent with the length difference in the spacer molecules BAI and IBAI. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first investigation of iso-butyl as a spacer layer in perovskites for PSCs. Cho et.al (35) used mixtures of n-butyl and iso-butylammonium cations for surface passivation treatments yielding a  PCE of $21.7\%$ mixed cation.
GIWAXS data of pristine and IBAI treated $\mathrm{FAPb}\mathrm{I}_{3}$ films (Figure 3a and 3b) confirm the formation of $\upalpha$ -FAPbI3 perovskite-phase in agreement with the XRR measurements. The additional 2D reflections (Figure 3b) arising from the $\ensuremath{2\mathrm{D}}\ensuremath{-}\ensuremath{\mathrm{IB}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{A}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}}$ overlayer are along the $q_{\mathrm{z}}$ axis indicating their orientation to be parallel to the substrate. With the radially integrated data over the measured $q$ -space, (Figure 3c) we can qualitatively estimate the amount of $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ and hexagonal phase FAPbI3 in the scattering volume. In the sample with $2\mathrm{D-IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ overlayer, a negligible content of the hexagonal polymorph and a smaller amount of excess $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ compared to the noncovered sample is observed. This establishes that FA from the ${\delta\mathrm{{-FAPbI}}_{3}}$ structure is consumed in the formation of the $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer. As compared to IBA2PbI4 $\scriptstyle(\mathrm{n=1})$ ) system, $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ passivating layer, a tailored 2D system, should demonstrate better charge transport while concurrently rendering the resulting PSC less susceptible to the moisture uptake.
## Charge-carrier dynamics
We further investigated the impact of the $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer on the optoelectronic properties of the $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI}}_{3}$ perovskite films using time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) as shown in Figure 3d Both the untreated and treated films show two distinct decay regimes. A very fast decay within the first 10 ns is followed by a slow mono-exponential decay. The fast decay at early times is caused by carrier diffusion due to the initial exponential excitation profile in combination with charge carrier trapping very likely caused by shallow traps on or near the surface (PbI2, $\delta\cdot$ -FAPbI3 non-perovskite phase or surface states). The IBAI treated film shows a slower decay by more than one order of magnitude within the first 10 ns compared to the untreated film. Since the carrier diffusion rate is the same in both films, we infer that the IBAI treatment alters the defect chemistry of the surface which leads to a significantly lower trap density. Also the first-order decay kinetics at times $>10$ ns are much slower for the treated than the untreated films. We applied numerical simulations to analyze the different decay kinetis. Details for this simulation can be found in Zhu et al (36). The single exponential decay can be caused by either SRH recombination with a recombination constant $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ or by surface recombinations with a surface recombination velocity S. Since both films have the same bulk material and, hence, the same $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ , the only difference lies in S. We considered two extreme cases for the IBAI treated film, where in the first case the film exhibits no bulk recombinations ( $\mathbf{\hat{k}}_{1}$ $=0$ ), i.e. the monoexponential decay is exclusively caused by surface recombination. Conversely, in the second case, we assume that the film exhibits no surface recombination $(\mathsf{S}=0)$ ), i.e. the decay is solely caused by SRH type bulk recombination.. From this, we calculated the difference in velocity of surface recombination for the two films to be $\Delta\mathrm{S}=32~\mathrm{cm/s}$ which is the same in both extreme cases. The details of this calculation can be found in Table S1 and the fitted curves are shown in Fig. 3d. This shows that IBAI treatment not only reduces the level of $\mathrm{Pb}\mathrm{I}_{2}$ and $\delta$ -FAPbI3 near the surface but it also efficiently passivates electronic trap states likely caused by these impurities at or near the surface of the perovskite film. This improvement in the optoelectronic film quality has an impact on the device performance and in particular in the $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{oc}}$ as will be shown in the following.
Photovoltaic performance and operational stability
In order to investigate the potential impact of combining the stepwise annealing with the introduction of a 2D $)-\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer on the photovoltaic performance we fabricated PSCs of the architecture FTO/compact-TiO2/mesoscopic- $\mathrm{\cdotTiO}_{2},$ /perovskite/2D-layer/spiro-OMeTAD/gold (Figure S10). Representative $J_{-}V$ curves of PSCs with and without 2D $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ perovskite layer are shown in Figure 4a, from which we deduce the photovoltaic metrics summarized in Table 2. The device using neat FAPbI3, subjected to treatment with MACl and stepwise sintering, showed a PCE of $20.4\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1053~\mathrm{{mV}}_{\mathrm{{t}}}$ , a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $25.87\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ and a fill factor of 0.772. Covering the perovskite film with the $2\mathrm{D-IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer enhances substantially all three PV metrics, the champion PSC achieving a PCE of $22.7\%$ , with $V_{0\mathrm{c}}=1112\mathrm{mV},J_{\mathrm{SC}}{=}25.4\mathrm{mAcm^{-2}}$ and $F F=80.5\%$ , which is one of the best efficiency values reported for all-iodide FAPbI3-based PSCs. We attribute the increase in the $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ , i.e. $\Delta V_{\mathrm{OC}}=191~\mathrm{mV}$ and $59\mathrm{mV}$ with regards to bare $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ films, with and without stepwise annealing, respectively, as well as the outstanding FF of $80.5\%$ to the effective suppression of $\delta\cdot$ -FAPbI3 phase formation together with the surface trap mitigation effect of the $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ overlayer. The surface treatment of the $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ perovskite with IBAI converts most of the $\delta$ -FAPbI3 into the 2D $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ surface layer, any residue being below the detection limit of our GIWAXS measurements. The removal of ${\delta\mathrm{-FAPbI}_{3}}$ prolongs the charge carrier lifetime as revealed by the TRPL measurements, enabling the high $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ values observed for PSCs that are endowed with an $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI_{3}/I B A_{2}F A P b_{2}I_{7}}}$ overlayer $(37)$ . In addition, the photovoltaic performance of $n$ -butyl ammonium iodide (BAI) surface treated $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ based perovskite is summarized in Figure S11. Champion cell with BAI treatment yielded PCE up to $21.8\%$ , as compared to $22.7\%$ with IBAI treatment and $20.4\%$ untreated. The statistic results prove superior photovoltaic performance of IBAI treated PSC as compared to BAI.
The inset of Figure 4a shows the tracking of the maximum power point (MPPT) for two cells with and without 2D-overlayer over the first 60 seconds. The power output (SPO) of the device endowed with the 2D layer reached a stabilized value of ${\sim}22.4\%$ . within $10\mathrm{{s}}$ This yields an SPO-to-PCE ratio of 0.99, implying a reliable solar energy output (38). Statistics of photovoltaic parameters corresponding to $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer–based PSCs are summarized and compared with those obtained for 2D-layer free PSCs in Figure 4c. Enhanced $V_{\mathrm{OC},F F}$ , and PCE values were obtained for over 25 perovskite devices endowed $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ overlayers, further revealing the effectiveness of using the 2D layer for passivating defects and for improving the photovoltaic performance.
Figure 4b shows incident photon to electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra of PSCs with and without $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ surface treatment layer. The integrated photocurrents are in excellent agreement with $J_{-}V$ measurements, their difference being $<2\%$ . To evaluate the effects of the film treatment on the long-term device stability, we record the operational stability of the PSCs by exposing unsealed devices at room temperature to full-sun illumination in flowing nitrogen gas while tracking their MPP (Figure 4d). After $700\mathrm{{h}}$ , FAPbI3 PSC endowed with the 2D IBA2FAPb2I7 layer retained over $95\%$ of its initial efficiency, proving excellent operational stability. In comparison, MACl treated PSCs without $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ surface layer retained merely $76\%$ of their initial efficiency. To further substantiate the superior operational stability of FAPbI3-based PSCs, we also performed MPP tracking experiment under simulated full-sun irradiance at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for FAPbI3–based PSC with and without 2D $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer. (Figure 4e). After over $500\mathrm{h}$ , the cell using a 2D $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ overlayer retained $84\%$ of its initial efficiency while the PCE of the bare $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ dropped to less than $26\%$ . The robust performance of $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI_{3}/I B A_{2}F A P b_{2}I_{7}}}$ -based PSCs results from the suppression of thed-FAPbI3 phase, better perovskite film quality, and superior moisture stability of $\alpha{\mathrm{-FAPbI_{3}/I B A_{2}F A P b_{2}I_{7}}}$ layer (Figure S12) (38-40). FAPbI3 films produced without the stepwise annealing and MACl treatment degrade severely within the first few hours of aging. Thus the new results obtained in our study set new benchmarks in terms of both efficiency and stability for FAPbI3 –based PSCs.
## Conclusions
In conclusion, using a stepwise annealing method, $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ –based perovskites have been stabilized by effectively suppressing the formation of $\delta$ -FAPbI3 non-perovskite phase. By exploiting the catalytic activity of MACl together with a surface coating of a molecularly tailored $\mathrm{IBA}_{2}\mathrm{FAPb}_{2}\mathrm{I}_{7}$ layer, a PCE approaching $23\%$ was achieved, which is amongst the highest values reported for pure FAPbI3–based PSCs. Such 2D coated PSCs exhibited superior operational stability under a heat stress of $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Our work provides an efficient and cost-effective way to stabilize and to improve the operational stability of FAPbI3 –based perovskite, paving the way for the industrialization of PSCs.
